/tmp/paper1910.q0oHfN/Equitable.tex:264: The $\fpt$ algorithm is very involved. We give a brief overview of the main ideas in our algorithm. Let $G$ be a graph and $X \subseteq V(G)$ of size at most $k$ such that $G \setminus X=(C,I)$ is a threshold graph. We start by guessing the coloring of $X$ in a solution and then  try to extend it to an equitable coloring of $G$. To extend a coloring of $X$ to $G$ we use the following key ideas (a) In any equitable coloring of $G$ the size of any color class is at most $k+2$. (b) As the size of $X$ is at most $k$, we can guess the color class sizes of colors used to color vertices of $X$. (c)  We use the neighborhood containment property of threshold graphs to assign new colors (colors not used in $X$) to color the clique and the independent set of $G \setminus X$ respectively.
